     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thanks for your reply Terrence. But I don&#39;t understand what you mean by univariate vs multivariate versions of Rubin&#39;s rules.</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>It should really be referred to as uni- vs. multiparameter tests.  I think it is self-explanatory, but read Enders (2010) book for a more thorough description and example applications.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\"><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424200705000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0LF31kUOnQ-c5SWdpYVxjK\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
